1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,627
Before we touch the controls, here's the challenge. A musician needs

2
00:00:05,627 --> 00:00:11,548
the right preset, the right settings, and a steady tempo. But when

3
00:00:11,548 --> 00:00:16,975
nothing happens, it's hard to tell where the connection failed. Did

4
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:21,908
the computer send the message? Did the device receive it?

5
00:00:22,908 --> 00:00:29,503
PulseStage gives us a way to check those steps. We can send a simple

6
00:00:29,503 --> 00:00:35,155
instruction, then watch a test receiver respond. Think of MIDI as a

7
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:39,394
remote control for musical equipment. It carries instructions, rather

8
00:00:39,394 --> 00:00:44,575
than the sound itself. Let's try it without any physical instruments.

9
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:50,721
Set the input to PulseStage Keyboard, and the output to PulseStage

10
00:00:50,721 --> 00:00:55,399
Pedal Test. These are two separate connections. The keyboard sends

11
00:00:55,399 --> 00:01:00,545
notes into our monitor. The command station sends settings to the

12
00:01:00,545 --> 00:01:04,755
reference pedal. Keyboard notes are not automatically passed through

13
00:01:04,755 --> 00:01:06,158
to the pedal.

14
00:01:07,158 --> 00:01:12,599
Click a piano key, then let go. Pressing it sends a note on

15
00:01:12,599 --> 00:01:17,621
instruction. Releasing it sends note off. You don't need to know how

16
00:01:17,621 --> 00:01:22,644
to play piano for this check. We are testing the messages. This

17
00:01:22,644 --> 00:01:25,992
keyboard does not include its own sound generator.

18
00:01:26,992 --> 00:01:33,227
Open Event monitor. Here we can see the note arrive, followed by its

19
00:01:33,227 --> 00:01:39,462
release. R X means received. T X means sent. This is useful when

20
00:01:39,462 --> 00:01:43,299
troubleshooting. A sent counter shows that PulseStage transmitted

21
00:01:43,299 --> 00:01:48,095
something. The received event gives us separate evidence that it

22
00:01:48,095 --> 00:01:48,575
arrived.

23
00:01:49,575 --> 00:01:54,992
Back in Control room, choose Control change, then Expression. Set the

24
00:01:54,992 --> 00:02:00,901
value to twenty, and click Send command. Now look at the reference

25
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:05,825
pedal. Its Expression display reads twenty. Simply changing the number

26
00:02:05,825 --> 00:02:11,242
in the editor does not send anything. We send it deliberately.

27
00:02:12,242 --> 00:02:17,672
Let's change that value to one hundred, and send again. The receiver

28
00:02:17,672 --> 00:02:22,649
updates its number and knob position. Those four pedal knobs are

29
00:02:22,649 --> 00:02:28,079
indicators of settings it received. They are not controls you can turn

30
00:02:28,079 --> 00:02:33,057
here. On real equipment, the meaning of each controller depends on

31
00:02:33,057 --> 00:02:34,867
that device's MIDI settings.

32
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:40,441
The metal button below the knobs represents a footswitch, like a

33
00:02:40,441 --> 00:02:45,016
button a musician presses with a foot. In this test receiver,

34
00:02:45,016 --> 00:02:50,007
controller sixty four turns its ring on or off. Send one hundred

35
00:02:50,007 --> 00:02:55,829
twenty seven for on, or zero for off. It is an indicator here, so

36
00:02:55,829 --> 00:02:57,908
clicking it won't operate it.

37
00:02:58,908 --> 00:03:05,118
Next, choose Program change, enter twenty four, and send. The receiver

38
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:10,762
shows preset twenty five. That difference is just numbering. MIDI

39
00:03:10,762 --> 00:03:16,972
starts at zero, while this receiver labels presets starting at one.

40
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:22,052
Always check the numbering convention before choosing a musician's

41
00:03:22,052 --> 00:03:22,617
preset.

42
00:03:23,617 --> 00:03:28,957
Set the tempo to one hundred twenty beats per minute, then press

43
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:33,407
Start. The reference receiver shows Running, and measures about one

44
00:03:33,407 --> 00:03:37,857
hundred twenty beats per minute from the incoming timing messages.

45
00:03:37,857 --> 00:03:42,752
When you're finished, press Stop. A real device must support external

46
00:03:42,752 --> 00:03:45,867
MIDI clock, and have that option enabled.

47
00:03:46,867 --> 00:03:52,308
A compatible physical MIDI device can appear in the connection lists

48
00:03:52,308 --> 00:03:57,750
once Windows recognizes it. Connect and power it, click Refresh, then

49
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:03,192
select its port. Some devices need a manufacturer's driver. Older MIDI

50
00:04:03,192 --> 00:04:09,128
sockets need a MIDI interface. An ordinary audio cable isn't a MIDI

51
00:04:09,128 --> 00:04:13,580
connection. We have not verified physical hardware in this

52
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:14,075
demonstration.

53
00:04:15,075 --> 00:04:20,379
The keyboard and reference pedal are already inside PulseStage. You

54
00:04:20,379 --> 00:04:26,213
don't need to open their older separate applications. But this virtual

55
00:04:26,213 --> 00:04:32,577
test still needs loop MIDI installed and running. MIDI OX is optional.

56
00:04:32,577 --> 00:04:38,411
The current developer version also needs Python and its packages. A

57
00:04:38,411 --> 00:04:42,124
finished Windows installer should include those application

58
00:04:42,124 --> 00:04:46,367
dependencies. That installer has not been built yet.

59
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:52,641
That's the basic workflow. Check the connections. Send one small

60
00:04:52,641 --> 00:04:57,916
change. Confirm that it arrived. Then test the actual musician's

61
00:04:57,916 --> 00:05:03,191
equipment with its documented settings. The written guide beside this

62
00:05:03,191 --> 00:05:10,048
video includes the same steps, so you can repeat them at your own

63
00:05:10,048 --> 00:05:10,575
pace.
